WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.170
Doctor Sleep brings Stephen King's novel to
the screen in a way that both honors the author's

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:09.900
original version of The Shining and manages
to reconcile it with Stanley Kubrick's vision.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.650
The result is a film that's a sequel to two
stories at once​, with its own mythology

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:16.400
and a multilayered ending.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.600
Abra spends much of the film getting to know
the extent of her own strength with the shining,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.500
and it's clear that she's quite pleased with
the things she can do.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:29.100
During their final encounter at the Overlook
Hotel, Rose tells Abra that she reminds her

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.560
of her younger self, because there's a darkness
in Abra that hasn't reached its full potential

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:34.060
yet.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:38.760
Will the darkness start to take her over as
she ages, or will she be able to handle it?

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.560
As we see from the look on her face in the
final shot, the film leaves it an open question.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.739
One of the earliest scenes in Doctor Sleep
establishes that even after he and his mother

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:52.880
escaped the Overlook, young Danny Torrance
was haunted by the ghosts who attacked him

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:53.380
there.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:58.100
They followed him from the hotel to his home
in Florida, and attempted to feed on him until

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:02.030
Dick Hallorann taught young Danny a trick
that would allow him to lock the ghosts away

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:03.000
in boxes.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:08.280
Dan has kept the Overlook ghosts locked away
in his own mind for years, but finally unleashes

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.520
them on Rose the Hat.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.140
The film's final scene makes it clear that,
while the Overlook is gone, the ghosts are

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:14.640
not.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.960
The woman from Room 237 has returned to haunt
Abra Stone's bathroom, trying to feed on her

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.240
shine now that she can't feed on Dan's.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.720
Abra clearly knows how to deal with the woman
thanks to Dan's mentorship, but what about

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.480
the rest of the Overlook ghosts?

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.860
When Dan and Abra meet in person for the first
time, Dan explains that a lot of people have

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.720
a little bit of a shine, but he's only met
a handful in his life who knew how to use

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:38.220
it.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:41.780
Abra is one of those people, of course, and
so is Dick Hallorann.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.600
Brad Trevor, the "Baseball Boy" Abra has a
vision of just before he dies, is another

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.659
with an uncanny gift that he is only just
beginning to understand when he's killed by

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:51.400
the True Knot.

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:56.540
The film makes it very clear that Abra's powerful
shine is the reason the True Knot is defeated,

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:00.520
because she was able to figure out who and
what they were in a way that no one else could.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.140
Now that she's defeated the Knot, she probably
won't stop using her power for good.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:08.080
Though there's darkness in her, there's also
tremendous light.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:13.080
It's easy to imagine her using her mighty
gift to track down others who shine, particularly

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.320
children, and make sure they're safe from
the places and things that want to eat what

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:17.820
shines.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:24.800
As Doctor Sleep begins, the True Knot is introduced
as a close knit, small group of psychic vampires

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.879
who roam the country under the radar and find
themselves slowly running out of the life

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.180
force they call "steam."

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:35.540
By the end, only Rose the Hat is left, and
in a confrontation at the Overlook, Dan taunts

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.000
her by proclaiming her to be the last one
standing.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.120
Rose responds that she's not actually the
last one.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.780
It may have been an empty boast designed to
scare Dan, but what if Rose is right about

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:46.640
people like her?

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.680
Are there still other Knots out there in this
world, roaming the landscape in search of

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:51.180
steam?

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.480
Are there more places like the Overlook that
will eat what shines?

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.040
Are there would-be Knots that are only just
beginning to learn how to consume steam, coming

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.180
together in new ways?

00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:04.700
More importantly, will people like that be
able to stay alive in a world where steam

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:05.780
is harder to come by?

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.420
Abra Stone will probably find out one day.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:13.560
In Stanley Kubrick's The Shining, we learn
very early on that the Overlook Hotel was

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:18.160
built atop a Native American burial ground,
which sets the stage for a legacy of violence

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.400
that carries on throughout the hotel's history.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:25.840
"The site is built on an Indian burial ground,
and I believe they actually had to repel some

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.560
Indian attacks while they were building it."

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:32.291
In Stephen King's original novel, the Overlook
burns down at the end of the story, but The

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.440
Shining film leaves it standing, seemingly
waiting for the next caretakers.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.819
The hotel finally burns to the ground at the
end of Doctor Sleep, but the ghosts don't

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:41.500
die with it.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:45.380
At least some of them are still present, trying
to follow Abra instead of Dan.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.080
With that in mind, what happens to the site
of the Overlook itself?

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:53.440
What will the owners of the land do with it
now that the rotted old hotel is gone?

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:54.660
Will it be turned into a mobile home lodge,
as it is in King's novel?

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.460
Will it be preserved as a heritage site in
an effort to calm down the negative energy

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:57.960
there?

00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.680
Will the peace be maintained?

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:04.060
Dan Torrance is well aware of the imprint
his father made on him.

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:08.000
He was so traumatized by what happened at
the Overlook that he didn't talk for some

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.720
time afterward, and he ultimately took up
his father's alcoholic habits later in life.

00:04:13.189 --> 00:04:17.400
The contrast between Dan and his father's
memory comes to a head in one of the most

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:22.740
stirring scenes in the film, when he meets
the ghost of his father now "Lloyd" the bartender

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.640
in the Gold Room at the ruined Overlook.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.530
They have a direct confrontation over a glass
of whiskey that Dan refuses to drink, while

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:33.520
his father's ghost fumes over the life he
claims his family stole from him.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:37.300
With the Overlook gone, is Jack Torrance able
to find peace?

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.640
He does find a version of it in Doctor Sleep
the novel, but the film leaves that question

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:42.140
open.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:46.440
Was he beyond saving, or did Dan free his
father by burning the hotel?

